Atlas overlay patch 0121: fed/fed-loader
Why: the fed:// loader and the exclusive http mapping (a Federate name is fed:// and nothing else, lead rule
 2026-11-05, ADR 0052). A fed:// navigation and every subresource of a fed:// page (styles, scripts, images,
 fetch()) get the Federate loader (chrome/browser/atlas/fed), which asks the helper atlas-fed over a local
 socket and hands the verified bytes and content type to the engine as an ordinary response (no cookies,
 nothing cached, GET and HEAD only). A navigation (page or frame) to http(s)://x.<Federate TLD> is redirected
 (307) to fed://x.tld first in the interceptor list; every other load a frame, worker or service worker makes
 to such a host (subresources, fetch, XHR) goes through a proxying URLLoaderFactory put first in the chain of
 WillCreateURLLoaderFactory and is answered by the Federate loader, never by the network service. There is
 no preference: the mapping is always on, and a failed fed:// load is an error, never a retry over http(s).
 A web page may not read Federate names (probe, fingerprint): a subresource, fetch, XHR or worker request
 to such a host is answered only when its initiator is a fed:// page; any other initiator gets the honest 403
 page. Frames and top-level navigations are allowed (they become fed:// documents).
 Every check lives in the helper; no resolver code is in the engine (ADR 0043 D4).
Files: chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client.cc, chrome/browser/BUILD.gn
Test filter: *AtlasFed* (chrome/browser/atlas/fed unit tests); journey: fed://home.fed shows the page; a changed signature shows the refusal page
Rebase risk: M (the three functions are hooked at their first lines; they are long and change often, the hunks do not)
Release: 0.13
License: GPL-3.0-or-later (hunks in files the inherited series patches)
Verified at: 156.0.8078.79, base tree plus the patches before it in the series (dev slot 4)

